  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14197172/assets/19f4f2d616fa648fcd4663be22b32ab18662fffa8e31f9bd44f0dd3b6da99208.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch region exists for the point $A.$ Hence, $A$ is a local maximum point.\n\n**Note**: For the point $A({x}_{A},{y}_{A},{z}_{A}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{A},{y}_{A})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $A$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14197172/assets/f0b16c2cb372280ec39915b0e8d4e396e68baec9a8e20edc5389ced079648836.png)",
